  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sat May 23 2026\n\n...THIS WEEKEND...\nBuilding high pressure aloft over the U.S. Southwest this\nafternoon, evident in steadily rising 500-mb heights and less\ncumulus development over the high terrain. Ridge aloft should be\nfully in place over the Southern Rockies by Sunday, leading to dry\nconditions a subtle warmup this weekend. Lowland highs will warm a\nfew degrees for Sunday, topping out in the lower 90s. This is\nstill right near normal for late-May. Surface winds remain quite \nlight out of the southwest 5 to 10 mph.\n\n...MONDAY...\nOrphan low pressure system off the California coast will move \nashore Monday morning. Negatively-tilted axis and strengthening \nof mid-level southerly flow over the region will push moisture \ncurrently stored over northern Mexico into New Mexico and West \nTexas. Precipitable Water increases from 0.4-0.5 Sunday up to \n0.8-0.9 Monday will be evident in greater alto cumulus coverage \nMonday morning.\n\nVort max at the base of the upper trough will lead to upper level\ndivergence and the development of scattered (60-80% coverage) \nshowers and thunderstorms during the afternoon hours. Currently, \nthe best chances favor areas along and east of the Rio Grande. For\nEl Paso's CWA, we're looking at Otero/Hudspeth Counties as the \nbest coverage. Surface mixing will lead to large \"inverted-V\" \nboundary layer profiles, thus strong outflow winds and generally \nlower-end rainfall rates will be common with any storms that form.\nThis could lead to gusty winds and blowing dust along the \nInternational Border and US-54 corridor near White Sands NP. \nCan't completely rule out strong to severe thunderstorms east of \nthe Rio Grande, as deep-layer shear will be sufficient, and low \nlevel backed flow will be in place over Otero and Hudspeth \nCounties. \n\n...TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY...\nMuch larger upper low off the Pacific NW will sit over the Great \nBasin rest of next week, absorbing Monday's orphan low and \nmaintaining a dry, southwest flow regime across Southern New \nMexico. Low level moisture will be quickly flushes east on \nTuesday. We may see some lingering isolated showers and \nthunderstorms along the eastern fringes of the forecast area \n(Sacramento/Guadalupe Mountains), but this will allow the return \nof dry conditions for the Rio Grande Valley. Dryline boundary \ndevelopment will sit just to our east, and models currently keep \nthis moisture off to the east over SE New Mexico and Trans-Pecos \nin West Texas. Temperatures will be warm, but near normal with \nlowland highs in the upper 80s/lower 90s.",
